      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_038db0f71abd5652477e62b845946f1ac819030d2b3ccb8f08ed81449c53d978",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Three distinct examples show that a limiting prescription is part of the mathematical object being evaluated: contour-height ordering fixes a conditionally convergent zero sum, principal-argument bookkeeping introduces a zero-counting staircase that cancels an apparent zeta phase in one kinematic limit, and a semi-local trace program requires globally coordinated principal-value normalizations rather than arbitrary local truncation.",
