    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_5ea9f45b96b5ef27107db7a153554c6fa56b08cadae770a0fd5d748cabc56838",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Aggregate zero counting closes missing information in two distinct ways. In finite RH verification, an exact total count matches critical-line sign-change witnesses to prove a bounded census; in a mollified zero-free-region detector, a uniform N(T) remainder controls the contribution of remote zeros as one error term among several analytic components.",
